# Batch files MUST check out with CRLF, on every platform.
#
# This is not a tidiness rule. cmd.exe reads a .bat by seeking through it, and
# with bare LF line endings its label resolution is unreliable: `goto :label`
# lands somewhere other than the label and execution falls into whatever
# follows. It does not error. Nothing in the output says a jump was missed.
#
# Measured here on 2026-09-18. run.bat was written LF-only and, given an empty
# stdin, its menu ran option 2 -- a live ledger merge that reads the venue and
# writes two files -- three times, while its own guard correctly reported that
# no input had been received. The guard logic was right; the goto that should
# have returned to the menu simply did not happen. Converting the file to CRLF
# fixed it with no change to a single statement.
#
# start-trading.bat has always been CRLF and has never shown this, which is
# why the difference went unnoticed until a second .bat was added.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf

# PowerShell has the same seek-based reader.
*.ps1 text eol=crlf
